\setlength{\parskip}{0.55em}
\setlist{nosep,leftmargin=1.4em}
\titleformat{\section}{\large\bfseries}{}{0pt}{}
\titlespacing*{\section}{0pt}{1.1em}{0.2em}
\newcommand{\file}[1]{\texttt{\small #1}}

\begin{document}

{\LARGE\bfseries Codacabana: a buyer that negotiates on total cost}\\[0.3em]
{\large Tacto Negotiation Arena, EHL Grand Finale, Munich, 10 to 11 October 2026}

Our agent buys for Hollerau Pumpenwerke. It negotiates by e-mail with Tacto's supplier bots and
closes one term sheet per case, or walks away. The score rewards total cost $C$ over the case
horizon, not unit price, so the agent plans payment, delivery, contract length, quality and index
clauses together with the price.

One rule shapes the whole design: \textbf{code owns every binding number, the model owns words.}
Prices, term sheets, the accept and the walk-away are computed and checked with Tacto's own
\file{total\_cost} and \file{must\_haves\_ok}. Language models read the supplier, prepare
arguments and write one paragraph per e-mail. Everything a model returns is picked from a fixed
list or checked by code, and every model call has a timeout with a code fallback, so a move
always goes out inside the arena's 60 seconds.

\section{The system}

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  font=\sffamily,
  box/.style={rounded corners=3pt, minimum width=3.4cm, minimum height=1.12cm, align=center,
              inner sep=3pt, line width=0.6pt},
  model/.style={box, fill=model, draw=modelline, text=white},
  code/.style={box, fill=code, draw=codeline, text=ink},
  arena/.style={box, fill=white, draw=ink, text=ink},
  wire/.style={draw=wire, line width=0.7pt, -{Stealth[length=5pt]}, rounded corners=2pt},
  bus/.style={draw=wire, line width=0.7pt},
]
\newcommand{\lbl}[2]{\textbf{##1}\\[-1pt]{\footnotesize ##2}}

% specialists
\node[model] (eco) at (0.5,0)   {\lbl{Economics}{Cost, indices}};
\node[model] (per) at (4.33,0)  {\lbl{Persona}{Letters, news}};
\node[model] (his) at (8.17,0)  {\lbl{History}{Past deals, contract}};
\node[model] (lev) at (12,0)    {\lbl{Leverage}{Quality, rivals}};

% rehearsal and lead
\node[model] (sup)  at (0.5,-2.5)  {\lbl{Supplier subagent}{Its arguments, weak points}};
\node[model] (buy)  at (6.25,-2.5) {\lbl{Buyer subagent}{Our answer to each}};
\node[model] (lead) at (12,-2.5)   {\lbl{Lead researcher}{Trades, claim order, tone}};

% code checks
\node[code] (bank)  at (0.5,-5)  {\lbl{Claim bank}{Facts cited by ID}};
\node[code] (brief) at (6.25,-5) {\lbl{Brief check}{Trades priced, figures cut}};

% live loop
\node[model] (ana) at (0.5,-7.5)  {\lbl{Live analyst}{Mood, tactic, asks}};
\node[code]  (eng) at (6.25,-7.5) {\lbl{Price engine}{Price, terms, accept or walk}};
\node[model] (wri) at (12,-7.5)   {\lbl{Writer}{One e-mail paragraph}};

\node[arena] (bot) at (6.25,-10) {\lbl{Supplier bot}{Replies within the arena}};

% after the case
\node[model, dashed] (deb) at (2.5,-12.4) {\lbl{Debrief}{Practice runs only}};
\node[code]  (mem) at (9.5,-12.4) {\lbl{Lessons memory}{Recalled by goal and case type}};

% specialists to the team
\draw[bus] (eco.south) -- (0.5,-1.25);
\draw[bus] (per.south) -- (4.33,-1.25);
\draw[bus] (his.south) -- (8.17,-1.25);
\draw[bus] (lev.south) -- (12,-1.25);
\draw[bus] (0.5,-1.25) -- (12,-1.25);
\draw[wire] (0.5,-1.25) -- (sup.north);
\draw[wire] (6.25,-1.25) -- (buy.north);
\draw[wire] (12,-1.25) -- (lead.north);

\draw[wire] (sup) -- (buy);
\draw[wire] (buy) -- node[right, font=\footnotesize, text=wire] {playbook} (brief);
\draw[wire] (lead.south) |- node[pos=0.75, above, font=\footnotesize, text=wire] {strategy} (brief.east);
\draw[wire] (brief) -- (eng);
\draw[wire] (bank.south) -- (0.5,-6.2) -| ([xshift=-1cm]eng.north);

\draw[wire] (ana) -- (eng);
\draw[wire] (eng) -- (wri);
\draw[wire] (wri.south) |- (bot.east);
\draw[wire] (bot.west) -| (ana.south);
\draw[wire] (bot.south) -- (6.25,-11.2) -| (deb.north);
\draw[wire] (deb) -- (mem);
\draw[wire] (mem.east) -- (14.5,-12.4) |- (lead.east);

% legend
\node[model, minimum width=0.5cm, minimum height=0.32cm] (l1) at (-0.95,-14) {};
\node[anchor=west, font=\footnotesize] at (l1.east) {Language model, output checked by code};
\node[code, minimum width=0.5cm, minimum height=0.32cm] (l2) at (5.6,-14) {};
\node[anchor=west, font=\footnotesize] at (l2.east) {Code, owns every number};
\node[arena, minimum width=0.5cm, minimum height=0.32cm] (l3) at (10.2,-14) {};
\node[anchor=west, font=\footnotesize] at (l3.east) {Tacto's arena};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The buyer as it runs at the submitted commit. The playbook also gives the live analyst
the arguments to recognise and the writer our prepared answer. The debrief is dashed because the
Live Final returns no score: the final plays with the lessons committed from practice.}
\end{figure}

\section{How it prepares}

Before a session opens, while no supplier clock runs, a research team gets 40 seconds per case
(\file{buyer/research.py}).

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Four specialists}, one per data group, each report up to four findings and who
  holds the leverage: economics, persona, history, leverage.
\item \textbf{The lead researcher} turns the findings and the outcomes of similar earlier cases
  into a strategy: up to two term trades, the order of our arguments, the tone, notes for the
  writer. It sets no price and no pace.
\item \textbf{A demo bidding} (\file{buyer/rehearsal.py}): a supplier subagent, in character,
  names the arguments it will bring and the weak points it hopes we miss. A buyer subagent
  answers each one. The result is a playbook of expected arguments and prepared answers.
\item \textbf{Code checks the brief.} A trade must be an allowed option of a negotiable field.
  Any line with a digit is dropped. A supplier we have no data on gets no trades and no weak
  points to press: we never write a supplier something our records do not show.
\end{itemize}

In parallel, code builds the supplier's profile, the claim bank, an estimate of its full cost and
cost of money, and the opening terms. Without a model, or when the team is late, the plain price
engine plays the case alone.

\section{What it reads in the data}

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}lX@{}}
\toprule
Source & What the agent takes from it \\
\midrule
Case file and quote & goal, need, horizon, must-haves, negotiable fields and their options, the
  supplier's standard terms \\
Should-cost, cost drivers, indices & an estimate of the supplier's full cost, moved by its
  drivers' index change; each driver's largest fall as an argument \\
Supplier master and intel & owner type and cash discount, which set its cost of money \\
Letters, notes, news, quote remarks & traits read by fixed patterns: cash need, full order book,
  near-cost pricing, hedged material \\
Past negotiations, contract, clauses & how far it usually moves, notice period, index clauses \\
Scorecards, forecast, orders, rival quotes & verified arguments for a lower price or better terms \\
Sunday release & read at runtime through the ID index, so new suppliers are covered \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}

Every argument we send is a claim from the claim bank: one sentence with the record's ID and its
figure, built by code from the data package (\file{buyer/claims.py}). The model cannot add a
figure. The agent branches on what the data says about a supplier, never on a case or supplier ID.

\section{How it decides what to offer}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Terms first.} Code prices every allowed combination of the non-price terms at the
  supplier's own valuation and picks the one with the lowest $C$ for us. A term that is cheap for
  us and valuable for the supplier, such as faster payment for one short of cash, buys price.
\item \textbf{Opening.} The first price is a share of the quote set by the category goal, from
  0.78 in leverage cases to 0.92 in stabilise cases, never below 0.75. The lead's trades go out as
  extra sheets next to the main one, each priced so that it costs us exactly the same. Whichever
  the supplier picks, we lose nothing and learn what it values.
\item \textbf{Each round.} We close 30\,\% of the gap to the supplier's counter. After a large
  first concession we hold instead, because it signals a deep floor. A patience budget keeps the
  gaps small for a supplier near its cost or fully booked.
\item \textbf{The live analyst} (\file{buyer/analyst.py}) reads every reply within 6 seconds and
  returns a mood, one of three tactics and the expected argument it recognised. ``Hold'' shrinks
  our step to 70\,\%, ``argue'' adds one more claim. It cannot name a price.
\item \textbf{Claims} go three in the first e-mail, then one per round, never repeated.
\item \textbf{Limits.} No sheet goes above the price where $C$ meets our alternative. In
  relationship goals the price never drops below the point where the relationship charge makes
  $C$ rise again.
\item \textbf{The e-mail.} The writer model answers the supplier's last e-mail in one paragraph
  with no digits and no IDs. Code appends the figures, the claims and the term sheets. A late or
  rejected paragraph is replaced by a template.
\end{itemize}

\section{When it closes or walks away}

The agent closes on the supplier's final offer, on its agreement, at the round limit, or 90
seconds before a wave's 8-minute cap. In a relationship goal it also stops once the supplier's
counter reaches our estimate of the cost-optimal price.

The close gate then looks only at standing sheets. A sheet passes if it has a price, keeps every
must-have and costs less than our alternative by \file{total\_cost}. With several bidders the
cheapest passing sheet wins. If none passes, the agent walks away. Before walking on a final word
it would refuse, it sends one sheet at its ceiling. A better alternative sheet from the supplier
is first sent back so that it becomes standing.

\section{What it learned about suppliers, and from where}

All of this comes from our own sandbox and practice sessions, logged with session IDs in
\file{docs/learnings.md}. Most findings rest on one to three sessions per supplier.

\begin{itemize}
\item Suppliers concede only after a move from us, and agree as soon as our sheet reaches the
  counter they would make. So our sheet stays below their counter until the end.
\item Every verified claim they accept lowers where they stop, most of it in round one. The first
  e-mail therefore carries our three strongest claims.
\item Patience ends at a cumulative gap of about 40\,\% between our sheets and their counters,
  much earlier for a supplier near its cost.
\item Threats, deadlines, bluffs and requests for a cost breakdown did not help. The agent uses
  none of them, which is also what we would want in front of a real supplier.
\item A model that moved prices cost score, so prices went back to code.
\end{itemize}

After each practice case a debrief model wrote one lesson, keyed on goal, case type, owner type
and traits, never on an ID. The lead researcher is shown the figures of the most similar earlier
runs, not the lesson text, because one run is a data point and not a rule. 64 such rows are
committed in \file{buyer/lessons.jsonl}.

\section{Results and limits}

On 11 October one practice session per hosted case gave ten deals out of ten and a mean score of
0.777. Our best scores per case on Saturday night averaged 0.776, rank 3 of 24 on the practice
board. That batch saw 121 of 127 subagent calls succeed; the six timeouts fell back to code.

One batch is not a paired comparison: we cannot show that the subagent team raises the score over
the plain price engine. The full-cost estimate rests on a should-cost with a stated error of 5 to
15\,\%, which is why the agent bounds it with the supplier's own offers.

\section{Run it}

Setup, environment variables and commands are in \file{README.md}. The Live Final runs with
\file{uv run buyer run --set final --parallel 10 -v}. The commit hash is in the submission on
ehl.gg.

\end{document}

